----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[R: a1-b1, P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, B: c1-d2, B: c1-e3, B: c1-f4, B: c1-g5, B: c1-h6, N: c3-a4, N: c3-b1, N: c3-b5, N: c3-d1, N: c3-d5, N: c3-e2, N: c3-e4, R: f1-e1, R: f1-d1, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, K: g1-h1, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "I'll analyze this chess position carefully.\n\n**Current Position Analysis:**\n- White has: King (g1), 2 Rooks (a1, f1), 1 Bishop (c1), 1 Knight (c3), 6 Pawns\n- Black has: King (e8), 2 Rooks (a8, h8), Queen (d8), 2 Bishops (c8, f8), 2 Knights (f7, c2), 7 Pawns\n\n**Key Observations:**\n1. Black has a dangerous knight on c2, very deep in White's position\n2. Black has captured some of White's pieces (missing Queen, Knight, Bishop, pawns)\n3. Material disadvantage for White (Black has Queen + extra Knight)\n4. White's king safety is compromised with the Black knight on c2\n5. Black's king hasn't castled yet (KBF0 = hasn't moved)\n\n**Evaluation of Promising Moves:**\n\n1. **N: c3-b1** - Attacks the Black knight on c2, forcing a trade. This removes Black's dangerous advanced piece but loses our active knight.\n\n2. **N: c3-d5** - Centralizes the knight with attacks on multiple squares (b6, c7, e7, f6). Strong central outpost, puts pressure on Black's position.\n\n3. **N: c3-e4** - Centralizes knight, controls key squares, potentially eyes f6 or d6.\n\n4. **B: c1-g5** - Develops bishop actively, but Black can easily develop and castle.\n\n5. **N: c3-b5** - Attacks toward c7, but less central control.\n\n**Reason:**\nWith a material disadvantage (Black has Queen advantage), I need to create immediate tactical threats. **N: c3-d5** is the strongest move because:\n- It centralizes my knight on an excellent outpost\n- Attacks c7 and e7, putting pressure on Black's undeveloped position\n- Controls key central squares\n- Black's king is still in the center and hasn't castled\n- Creates potential tactical opportunities\n- Better than trading knights when down material - I need active play\n\n**Answer: N: c3-d5**"}
